# ffi/logs/rust/stage3-zeroed-group.log
# W3: the ZEROED-GROUP variant of the element fill, built as an ARM.
# ABI v1 open decision 9 candidate. 2026-09-18
#
# CONFIGURATION (R7)
#   machine        4 vCPU Intel Xeon @ 2.80GHz, 15 GB, Ubuntu 24.04.4, Linux 6.18.44 x86_64,
#                  container, NOT an isolated host. No CPU pinning, no governor control.
#   rust           rustc/cargo 1.94.1, profile release (opt-level 3, lto off)
#   MSRV declared  1.88. NOT VERIFIED: no 1.88 toolchain exists in this container.
#   incumbent      prost 0.14.4, carried as the in-process control on every row
#   LINKAGE        cdylib (libak_core.so), dynamic linker. Not a static link.
#   guard          on
#   content        ASCII.
#   class          M1 (P1.2, P1.3), M2 (P2.2, P2.5) and M3 (P3.1) are all REAL messages
#                  (design/SHAPES.md). No control payloads here.
#
# THE PROPOSAL, AND WHAT IT ACTUALLY TRADES
#   The element-group array is zeroed once per chunk with a memset and the host then assigns
#   only the fields that are not at their default, instead of the total fill ABI v1 section 6
#   requires.
#
#   **One correction to the framing, and it matters for reading the numbers.** Section 6 says
#   the total fill buys "the codec does not reset the element group between elements, worth
#   5.4 ns per ResultRaw and 24.4 per TaskDetailed". This proposal does NOT give that back:
#   the array is the HOST's chunk buffer, so under this variant the codec still never resets
#   anything. What changes is only the host's fill -- an unconditional store of every field
#   becomes a bulk memset plus a conditional store of the fields that differ. The reset moved
#   from a per-field reset the codec would have done to a bulk memset the host does, and a
#   bulk memset is cheaper per byte than scattered stores. That is why it can win at all, and
#   it is why the 5.4/24.4 figure is the right THRESHOLD to judge the cost against but is not
#   the cost being paid back.
#
# WHAT IS BUILT, AND WHAT IS NOT
#   The generator emits `fill_<msg>_sparse` for every group type and `loop_<root>_<slot>_zeroed`
#   plus `encode_into_<root>_zeroed` for every top-level repeated-message slot, ALONGSIDE the
#   default path. **Nothing the default path uses changed**: the conformance run and the
#   boundary-call counts in sections 1 and 2 below are the same figures as before, to the digit.
#   Only the TOP-LEVEL element group is filled this way; a nested loop inside an element keeps
#   the total fill, so on M2 this prices the outer `TaskDetailed` group and not the inner runs.
#   Encode only: decode has no host-side group fill to change.
#
# THE RULE THE SPARSE FILL MUST NOT BREAK
#   For an EXPLICIT-PRESENCE field the test is PRESENCE, not value: `Some(0)` and `Some("")`
#   are at their default value and must still be written, or present-and-zero silently becomes
#   absent. For an implicit-presence field the test is the value, because proto3 says an
#   implicit zero is not on the wire. Getting that backwards is the defect this variant invites,
#   and it is what positive control 3 below is for.
#
# THREE POSITIVE CONTROLS: THE CASES THAT MUST FAIL (D13)
#   A zeroed arm that silently fell back to the total fill would pass byte identity and measure
#   the same, which is exactly the situation the branch's rule is about. So each was broken on
#   purpose, one line at a time, in `crates/harness/src/generated/binding.rs`, and the tree was
#   restored with `python3 gen/generate.py` afterwards. Transcripts:
#
#   CONTROL 1 -- drop `ResultRaw.name` from the sparse fill:
#     delete: if !o.name.is_empty() { d.name = str_arg(&o.name, tc.0); }
#     P1.1  core-ffi-rust      858     858 ok      ok
#     P1.1  core-ffi-zeroed    858     818 DIFFER  DIFFER
#     P1.2  core-ffi-rust   218121  218121 ok      ok
#     P1.2  core-ffi-zeroed 218121  208132 DIFFER  DIFFER
#     P1.3  core-ffi-zeroed    605     605 ok      ok      <- P1.3's names are all empty
#     -> the M1 zeroed path is the one being exercised.
#
#   CONTROL 2 -- drop `TaskDetailed.owner_pod_id` from the sparse fill:
#     P2.1  core-ffi-zeroed   1037     999 DIFFER  DIFFER
#     P2.2  core-ffi-zeroed 540422  521422 DIFFER  DIFFER
#     P2.3  core-ffi-zeroed 647024  642274 DIFFER  DIFFER
#     P2.4  core-ffi-zeroed 979465  976425 DIFFER  DIFFER
#     P2.5  core-ffi-zeroed  19632   18872 DIFFER  DIFFER
#     -> the M2 zeroed path is the one being exercised, and P2.5 is NOT fully absent.
#
#   CONTROL 3 -- turn the presence test into a value test on `Probe.opt_count`:
#     if let Some(v) = o.opt_count { if v != 0 { ... } }
#     P3.1  core-ffi-rust    12097   12097 ok      ok
#     P3.1  core-ffi-zeroed  12097   12059 DIFFER  DIFFER
#     -> present-and-zero is load-bearing, and M3 catches it. This is the defect the
#        variant invites and the reason M3 is in the conformance list at all.
#
# MEASUREMENT HAZARDS THIS TABLE IS EXPOSED TO (R9)
#   1. Container, shared vCPUs, no pinning. Spread is printed per row and reaches 50 percent
#      on some encode rows; six runs are printed and the ranges below are over all six.
#   2. The per-element deltas are differences of two numbers of the same size. On P1.2 and
#      P2.5 the delta is inside the spread of zero and should be read as "no measurable
#      change", not as the sign of the midpoint.
#   3. This is one host language. The proposal's cost is a conditional store per field, and
#      what a branch costs depends on the host: a JIT with a profile may do better, and a
#      host whose fields are boxed may do worse. A Rust figure here does not settle it for
#      the managed slices.
#   4. Crossing counts are unchanged by the variant: it changes what the host writes into the
#      group before each `ak_elem` call and not how many calls there are.
#
# WHAT THIS LOG SAYS, BEFORE THE NUMBERS
#   1. IT WINS DECISIVELY ON M1's ABSENT PATH and removes the encode inversion there.
#      P1.3 goes from 1.108-1.188 of prost under the total fill to 0.815-0.857 under the
#      zeroed group: 0.719 to 0.766 of the total fill's own time, 4.06 to 4.98 ns saved per
#      element.
#   2. IT COSTS NOTHING MEASURABLE WHERE IT WAS EXPECTED TO LOSE. P1.2, every field
#      populated, is 0.986 to 1.014 of the total fill: -1.66 to +1.70 ns per element, inside
#      the spread of zero.
#   3. AND P2.2 -- THE SHAPE THE CONTROL PLANE ACTUALLY MOVES, AND THE ROW THAT DECIDES IT --
#      DOES NOT LOSE EITHER. It is 0.970 to 0.985 of the total fill, a consistent small
#      SAVING of 13 to 26 ns per element across all six runs. `TaskDetailed` carries 27
#      fields and many of them are at their default even on a fully populated payload, and
#      the per-element cost on M2 is dominated by ten crossings and the inner runs rather
#      than by the outer group fill.
#   4. P2.5, M2's absent path, shows no measurable change (-0.42 to +0.17 ns per element).
#      Positive control 2 explains why: P2.5 is not fully absent, so there is little for a
#      sparse fill to skip.
#   5. SO THE CONDITION SET FOR DECISION 9 IS MET on every payload measured: it wins on the
#      absent path and costs less than 5.4 ns per element everywhere else -- the worst case
#      measured is +1.70 ns, and two of the four rows are savings.
#   6. WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it does not help M2's absent path, it is encode-only, and it
#      applies to the top-level element group only. The nested groups inside an element keep
#      the total fill and were not measured separately.
#
